Here are a few ways to make a basic fetch request in JavaScript:

Using fetch:

```js
fetch('https:\/\/example.com\/movies.json')
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data));
```

Using async\/await: 

```js
async function getMovies() {
  const response = await fetch('https:\/\/example.com\/movies.json');
  const data = await response.json();
  console.log(data);
}
```

With options object:

```js 
fetch('https:\/\/example.com\/movies.json', {
  method: 'GET'
})
.then(response => response.json())
.then(data => console.log(data));
```

Post request:

```js
fetch('https:\/\/example.com\/movies', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application\/json'
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    title: 'A New Hope' 
  })
})
.then(response => response.json())
.then(data => console.log(data)); 
```

Some key points:

- Use fetch to make the request
- Chain .then() to handle response 
- Call .json() to parse JSON response
- Use async\/await for cleaner code with promises

You can also add error handling, change HTTP methods, pass headers\/body etc.

